WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.787
Welcome to this Java tutorial.

00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:05.706
You'll use JUnit to check a method's normal

00:00:05.706 --> 00:00:08.421
result, a boundary case, and an expected

00:00:08.421 --> 00:00:09.100
exception.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.816
You'll also interpret a test summary and

00:00:11.816 --> 00:00:14.803
deliberately challenge an assertion to check

00:00:14.803 --> 00:00:17.315
that the tests can detect a mismatch.

00:00:18.067 --> 00:00:21.351
A program can still compile after a change that

00:00:21.351 --> 00:00:22.749
breaks its behavior.

00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:25.754
Automated tests make important expectations

00:00:25.754 --> 00:00:28.339
repeatable, so a team can check those

00:00:28.339 --> 00:00:31.065
expectations again as its code changes.

00:00:31.065 --> 00:00:34.070
Each test gives evidence about a particular

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:37.075
case; passing tests do not prove that every

00:00:37.075 --> 00:00:38.542
possible input works.

00:00:39.231 --> 00:00:42.575
Our campus workshop needs one label for each kit

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:43.620
in every group.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.965
Two groups with three kits each need six labels.

00:00:46.965 --> 00:00:48.985
Zero groups need zero labels.

00:00:48.985 --> 00:00:52.260
Negative counts are invalid, so the method must

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.256
reject them with a clear exception message.

00:00:55.924 --> 00:00:58.885
We'll build three tests for those rules, then

00:00:58.885 --> 00:01:00.069
run them together.

00:01:00.069 --> 00:01:02.767
A successful baseline should report three

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:04.477
succeeded and zero failed.

00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:07.306
After reading that result, we'll change one

00:01:07.306 --> 00:01:10.136
expectation and use the next summary to see

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:12.899
whether the test detects the disagreement.

00:01:13.663 --> 00:01:16.814
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:16.814 --> 00:01:17.547
Main.java.

00:01:17.547 --> 00:01:20.845
This lesson's prepared project includes JUnit

00:01:20.845 --> 00:01:24.142
and the Run configuration, so we can focus on

00:01:24.142 --> 00:01:25.388
testing behavior.

00:01:30.641 --> 00:01:33.497
First, let's import the test and assertion

00:01:33.497 --> 00:01:36.692
tools, followed by the launcher tools that will

00:01:36.692 --> 00:01:39.616
run our test class and collect its results.

00:02:21.502 --> 00:02:24.736
These imports make library types available by

00:02:24.736 --> 00:02:26.030
their short names.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.618
Importing JUnit does not run a test.

00:02:28.618 --> 00:02:31.852
We still need a method to check, test methods

00:02:31.852 --> 00:02:34.871
that state expectations, and a runner that

00:02:34.871 --> 00:02:35.877
executes them.

00:02:35.918 --> 00:02:37.647
Let's define labelCount.

00:02:37.647 --> 00:02:40.817
Its two inputs represent groups and kits per

00:02:40.817 --> 00:02:41.249
group.

00:02:41.249 --> 00:02:44.562
The guard rejects either negative count before

00:02:44.562 --> 00:02:45.643
multiplication.

00:03:06.931 --> 00:03:10.137
For valid counts, the product is the number of

00:03:10.137 --> 00:03:10.625
labels.

00:03:10.625 --> 00:03:13.830
For an invalid count, IllegalArgumentException

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.966
reports that the caller broke the input rule.

00:03:16.966 --> 00:03:19.963
The message is part of the behavior we will

00:03:19.963 --> 00:03:20.381
check.

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:23.506
Now let's start the test class and add our

00:03:23.506 --> 00:03:25.201
ordinary positive case.

00:03:25.201 --> 00:03:28.665
The Test annotation marks a method for JUnit to

00:03:28.665 --> 00:03:29.328
discover.

00:03:29.328 --> 00:03:32.645
The assertion compares an expected value with

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:34.635
the method's actual result.

00:03:48.844 --> 00:03:52.121
Six is the expected value; the call with two and

00:03:52.121 --> 00:03:54.305
three produces the actual value.

00:03:54.305 --> 00:03:57.377
If they differ, this assertion makes the test

00:03:57.377 --> 00:03:57.718
fail.

00:03:57.718 --> 00:04:00.722
The test's name describes the behavior being

00:04:00.722 --> 00:04:03.999
checked, while the public constructor lets JUnit

00:04:03.999 --> 00:04:05.569
create the test object.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:08.659
Zero sits at the edge of the valid counts.

00:04:08.659 --> 00:04:12.083
Let's add a separate boundary test so this case

00:04:12.083 --> 00:04:15.142
remains visible even if the implementation

00:04:15.142 --> 00:04:15.725
changes.

00:04:27.532 --> 00:04:30.948
The expected result is zero because there are no

00:04:30.948 --> 00:04:32.086
groups to label.

00:04:32.086 --> 00:04:34.720
Keeping this in a separate test helps

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.566
distinguish an ordinary calculation from

00:04:37.566 --> 00:04:40.982
behavior at the edge of the allowed input range.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:44.189
Our third test checks rejection of a negative

00:04:44.189 --> 00:04:44.611
count.

00:04:44.611 --> 00:04:47.559
Let's include both a failure when the call

00:04:47.559 --> 00:04:50.719
returns normally and a message check when the

00:04:50.719 --> 00:04:52.755
expected exception is caught.

00:05:23.663 --> 00:05:26.411
The negative call should jump into the catch

00:05:26.411 --> 00:05:26.786
block.

00:05:26.786 --> 00:05:29.347
If it returns instead, the fail assertion

00:05:29.347 --> 00:05:30.908
prevents a false success.

00:05:30.908 --> 00:05:33.594
In the catch block, assertEquals checks the

00:05:33.594 --> 00:05:34.094
message.

00:05:34.094 --> 00:05:36.905
A different exception type is not accepted by

00:05:36.905 --> 00:05:37.717
this handler.

00:05:37.717 --> 00:05:40.465
Together, these paths check more than merely

00:05:40.465 --> 00:05:43.338
calling the method and hoping an error occurs.

00:05:43.407 --> 00:05:46.529
The tests are defined, but they have not run.

00:05:46.529 --> 00:05:49.790
Let's add the application entry point and build

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.982
a discovery request selecting this test class.

00:06:18.026 --> 00:06:21.482
The request identifies the tests we want JUnit

00:06:21.482 --> 00:06:22.083
to find.

00:06:22.083 --> 00:06:25.164
Next, a summary listener will collect the

00:06:25.164 --> 00:06:25.765
results.

00:06:25.765 --> 00:06:29.071
We'll open a launcher session, register that

00:06:29.071 --> 00:06:31.626
listener, and execute the request.

00:07:00.364 --> 00:07:03.417
Execute is the step that runs the selected

00:07:03.417 --> 00:07:03.853
tests.

00:07:03.853 --> 00:07:07.197
Try-with-resources closes the launcher session

00:07:07.197 --> 00:07:07.924
afterward.

00:07:07.924 --> 00:07:11.050
The individual test order is not prescribed

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:14.249
here; our summary counts outcomes across the

00:07:14.249 --> 00:07:15.339
selected tests.

00:07:15.428 --> 00:07:18.494
Finally, let's print the succeeded and failed

00:07:18.494 --> 00:07:21.559
counts from the collected summary, then click

00:07:21.559 --> 00:07:23.603
Run to establish our baseline.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:53.310
Three tests succeeded and none failed.

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:56.354
That result covers the positive product, the

00:07:56.354 --> 00:07:59.676
zero boundary, and rejection of a negative group

00:07:59.676 --> 00:08:01.890
count with the expected message.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:05.142
It is our baseline for these three cases, not a

00:08:05.142 --> 00:08:07.080
guarantee about every input.

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:10.512
Let's challenge the positive test by changing

00:08:10.512 --> 00:08:13.631
only its expected value from six to seven.

00:08:13.631 --> 00:08:16.824
The method and its two inputs will stay the

00:08:16.824 --> 00:08:17.195
same.

00:08:25.618 --> 00:08:28.779
Before running, predict the two summary counts.

00:08:28.779 --> 00:08:31.940
The calculation still uses two groups and three

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:34.967
kits per group, but one assertion now expects

00:08:34.967 --> 00:08:35.371
seven.

00:08:35.371 --> 00:08:37.725
Which tests should agree with their

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:38.599
expectations?

00:08:38.599 --> 00:08:41.693
Pause here and reason through all three cases.

00:08:49.849 --> 00:08:53.263
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:08:53.263 --> 00:08:54.899
JUnit's actual summary.

00:09:00.157 --> 00:09:02.438
Two tests succeeded and one failed.

00:09:02.438 --> 00:09:05.175
The positive test expected seven while the

00:09:05.175 --> 00:09:06.478
method returned six.

00:09:06.478 --> 00:09:08.890
The other expectations still matched.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:11.431
This deliberate mismatch checks that an

00:09:11.431 --> 00:09:14.429
assertion can detect disagreement; it is not a

00:09:14.429 --> 00:09:17.492
regression, because we did not break previously

00:09:17.492 --> 00:09:19.382
correct application behavior.

00:09:19.467 --> 00:09:23.005
Now let's restore the correct expectation of six

00:09:23.005 --> 00:09:25.142
and run the same tests again.

00:09:39.001 --> 00:09:42.146
The summary returns to three succeeded and zero

00:09:42.146 --> 00:09:42.614
failed.

00:09:42.614 --> 00:09:45.291
We repaired the test expectation without

00:09:45.291 --> 00:09:46.629
changing the method.

00:09:46.629 --> 00:09:49.707
A future unintended behavior change would be a

00:09:49.707 --> 00:09:52.852
regression if it broke behavior that had worked

00:09:52.852 --> 00:09:55.930
before; keeping useful tests helps detect that

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:57.001
kind of problem.

00:09:57.116 --> 00:09:59.839
You used assertions to make three behavior

00:09:59.839 --> 00:10:02.692
rules executable, included both paths needed

00:10:02.692 --> 00:10:05.544
for a reliable exception test, and separated

00:10:05.544 --> 00:10:07.684
defining tests from running them.

00:10:07.684 --> 00:10:10.666
For a transfer question, what extra test would

00:10:10.666 --> 00:10:12.417
check a negative kit count?

00:10:12.417 --> 00:10:15.334
In the notebook, you will also add a boundary

00:10:15.334 --> 00:10:16.566
test for zero kits.
